## Overview

In this exercise, we

## Getting Started in R

1.  Create a new project under **File - New Project**. Set up a new directory for easy navigation and launch the project.
2.  Next, create a new folder named **In-class_Ex**.
3.  Inside this folder, create a subfolder named **In-class_Ex01**. Then, create a new Quarto document and save it in the **In-class_Ex01** subfolder. Name the file **In-class_Ex01.qmd**.
4.  Usually we also create another subfolder named **data** for storing datasets used during the analysis. Within the **data** folder, create three subfolders: **geospatial**, **aspatial (attribute table)**, and **rds** for saving different types of data.

## Installing and Loading the R packages

There are **4** packages used in this exercise.

| Package | Description |
|------------------------------------|------------------------------------|
| [sf](https://r-spatial.github.io/sf/) | Handles importing, managing, and processing vector-based geospatial data |
| [tidyverse](https://www.tidyverse.org/) | A collection of R packages for data wrangling and visualisation, including **readr**, **dplyr**, and **ggplot2**. |
| [tmap](https://r-tmap.github.io/tmap/) | Creates cartographic quality static and interactive thematic (choropleth) maps. |
| [ggstatsplot](https://indrajeetpatil.github.io/ggstatsplot/) | An extension of **ggplot2**.Provides graphical displays of statistical tests. |


The code chunk below loads the required package into R.

```{r}
pacman::p_load(tidyverse, sf, tmap, ggstatsplot)
```

If the required packages are not yet installed, use the function `install.packages()` to install them first. Remember to enclose the package names in double quotation marks ("") and separate them with commas (,) if installing more than one package.

## Working with Master Plan Planning Sub-zone Data

1. As mentioned earlier, create a subfolder named data inside the In-class_Ex01 folder.
2. If necessary, visit [data.gov.sg](data.gov.sg) and download the **Master Plan 2014 Subzone Boundary (Web) dataset** from the portal in both the ESRI Shapefile and KML file formats.


The code chunk below imports the **Master Plan 2014 Subzone Boundary (Web) dataset** in both **Shapefile** and **KML** formats, and saves them as sf (simple features) data frames. Note that the import syntax differs between the two formats: when importing a Shapefile, the file extension .shp is not required, while for a KML file, the full file name including the extension must be specified in the relative directory path.

```{r}
mpsz14_shp <- st_read(dsn = "data/", layer = "MP14_SUBZONE_WEB_PL")
```
However, we encounter error message when importing the kml file.

```{r}
# mpsz14_kml <- st_read("data/MasterPlan2014SubzoneBoundaryWebKML.kml")
```

After receiving an error that the Web KML version cannot be opened, the code chunk below confirms that R can process KML files correctly.

```{r}
st_drivers()%>% filter(grepl("KML", name))
```
The code chunk below lists all the files in kml exension in the data folder.

```{r}
list.files("data/", pattern = "\\.kml$", full.names = TRUE)
```
We also tested another 2014 Master Plan KML (“No Sea”), but it cannot be read either.

```{r}
# mpsz14_kml <- st_read("data/MasterPlan2014SubzoneBoundaryNoSeaKML.kml")
```

The 2019 version loads successfully, confirming that the issue lies with the 2014 KML file format.

```{r}
mpsz19_kml <- st_read("data/MasterPlan2019SubzoneBoundaryNoSeaKML.kml")
```

**Task**: 

The code chunk exports mpsz14_shp sf data.frame into kml file save the output in data sub-folder and names the output file MP14_SUBZONE_WEB_PL. `delete_dsn = TRUE` checks if a file named MP14_SUBZONE_WEB_PL.kml already exists in the data folder, if so, it will delete it before writing a new kml file.

```{r}
st_write(mpsz14_shp, 
        "data/MP14_SUBZONE_WEB_PL.kml",
        delete_dsn = TRUE)
```

## Working with Pre-school Location Data

visit [data.gov.sg](data.gov.sg) and download **Pre-Schools Location** from the portal in both the kml and geojson files.

The code chunk imports Pre-Schools Location in kml and geojson and saves them in sf simple features data frame.

```{r}
preschool_kml <- st_read("data/PreSchoolsLocation.kml")
```

```{r}
preschool_geojson <- st_read("data/PreSchoolsLocation.geojson")
```



